{
 "id": "2022-shi-perennial",
 "name": "Perennial Plantings: Preference Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the preference rating task (Experiment B) in Shi, Zhang, Shen, Chen, Xu, Fu, Su & Xia (2022), \"Designing Perennial Landscapes: Plant form and Species Richness Influence the Gaze Perception Associated with Aesthetic Preference\", Land 11(10):1860, doi:10.3390/land11101860. The 36 images are the authors' photomontages of perennial plant combinations, printed in Figure 3 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0). They are used with credit for research, and the template is taken down if the authors object.\n\nWhat participants do: all 36 photomontages appear in random order. Before each one a dot is shown in the centre of the screen for 3 s, then the picture for 10 s, then the participant rates their preference from 1 to 10 and is asked to go with their first impression. The page ends with gender, age and whether they work or study in planting or landscape design.\n\nImages: <form>_<species>. Form: C = conical, M = mounding, V = vertical, CM / CV / MV = mixes of two forms. Species = number of perennial species (4 to 9). The paper's plant arrangement pattern follows the species number: 4 = E, 5 = F, 6 = C, 7 = B, 8 = A, 9 = D.\n\nOriginal study: Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China. Experiment B had 43 participants (22 of them professionals in planting landscape design) with a Tobii TX300 eye tracker. Analysis: preference and gaze measures by plant form and species richness, gender and professional background; correlation of preference with fixation count and duration. Non-professionals gave clearly higher ratings than professionals; men and women did not differ.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) No eye tracking; online on any screen instead of a lab monitor with the pictures framed on a 1920 × 1080 black background. (2) The pictures come from the printed figure (about 520 × 260 pixels each) and the arrangement letters printed in each corner were removed. (3) The paper does not say whether the rating followed each picture or came after all of them; here each picture is rated right after its 10 s viewing. (4) Scale end labels \"lowest preference\" and \"highest preference\" were added; the paper only says that 1 to 10 means increasing preference. (5) The paper's sociodemographic form is not printed; gender, age and a planting-design background question were reconstructed from what the paper reports. (6) Experiment A (free viewing without ratings) is not included.",
